 The signal still decays, but at a rate that is exponentially small in $r$. Two input histories that differ only in this bit agree on their last $t$ inputs, yet their echo states remain at trace distance at least $q(1-2p_r)^t$; with the bound on $p_r$, this gives Theorem~\ref{thm:main}(ii). At $q=0.9$ and $\varepsilon=0.01$, the single-qubit memory depth exceeds 300 time steps for $k=5$ and two million for $k=15$. For the odd values $k=5,\ldots,15$ in Fig.~\ref{fig:attain}(b), each pair of additional input qubits increases this depth by more than a factor of five. The same copies without majority vote keep the bit for at most 53 time steps; the fresh qubits extend memory because the reservoir uses them for repair.
 
-\runin{Standard reservoirs operate far below the limit.}The simulations do not test the bound; they locate standard reservoirs relative to it. We simulated transverse-field Ising reservoirs with random all-to-all couplings, the workhorse of QRC~\cite{Fujii2017,Nakajima2019,MartinezPena2021}, and Haar-random unitaries at $q=0.9$, and estimated their memory depth $W$ at $\varepsilon=0.01$~\cite{SM}. Within these ensembles, size does not extend their memory [Fig.~\ref{fig:attain}(c)]. With one input qubit ($k=1$), the Ising reservoir remembers 16 to 18 time steps for every size from $n=3$ to $9$ qubits, while the Haar reservoir forgets faster as it grows ($W$ drops from 9 to 4 time steps). Nor do more input qubits help [Fig.~\ref{fig:attain}(c)]~\cite{SM}. At fixed size ($n=8$), raising $k$ from 1 to 4 lowers $W$ from 18 to 8 time steps (Ising) and from 4 to 3 (Haar), and the decrease persists when the additional input qubits are reinitialized in $\ket0$. Both stay below an idle qubit that merely holds the input ($W=43$ time steps), whereas the error-correcting reservoir of the same size ($n=8$, $k=5$) remembers over 300 time steps.
+\runin{Standard reservoirs operate far below the limit.}The simulations do not test the bound; they locate standard reservoirs relative to it. We simulated transverse-field Ising reservoirs with random all-to-all couplings, the workhorse of QRC~\cite{Fujii2017,Nakajima2019,MartinezPena2021}, and Haar-random unitaries at $q=0.9$, and estimated their memory depth $W$ at $\varepsilon=0.01$~\cite{SM}. Within these ensembles, size does not extend the memory [Fig.~\ref{fig:attain}(c)]: with one input qubit, the Ising reservoir remembers 16 to 18 time steps for every size from $n=3$ to $9$, and the Haar reservoir forgets faster as it grows ($W$ drops from 9 to 4). More input qubits raise the ceiling but, in generic dynamics, lower the memory, because reinitialization also erases what those qubits had stored: at $n=8$, raising $k$ from 1 to 4 lowers $W$ from 18 to 8 time steps (Ising) and from 4 to 3 (Haar), even when the additional qubits are reinitialized in $\ket0$~\cite{SM}. Both stay below an idle qubit that merely holds the input ($W=43$), whereas the error-correcting reservoir of the same size ($n=8$, $k=5$) remembers over 300 time steps. The difference lies not in the supply of fresh qubits but in what the dynamics does with them.
 
 \runin{Beyond reservoirs: an entropy budget.}For other noise, the result becomes an entropy budget~\cite{SM}. Any additional channel with budget $b$ adds $b$ bits, and the upper bound holds with $k$ replaced by $k+b$. Unital noise (Pauli errors, dephasing, coherent errors, crosstalk) and projective measurements whose outcomes only enter the readout~\cite{Mujal2023} have $b=0$; since every unital qubit channel with full-rank Choi matrix contains a depolarizing component, the theorem covers generic independent unital noise~\cite{SM}. Resets of $m$ qubits count like $m$ input qubits. Amplitude damping with probability $0<\gamma<1$ adds $n\log_2(1+\gamma)$ bits, and relaxing idle qubits then act as a refrigerator~\cite{BenOr2013} that lets a reservoir with two input qubits remember for a time exponential in $n$~\cite{SM,Hoeffding1963}. In the language of error correction, a measurement-free recovery that consumes $k$ fresh ancillas per round is, with its data qubits, a system in $\mathcal N$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:scope}]: it keeps a logical bit for at most $\exp[O_q(k)]$ rounds beyond the washout of its initial state, whatever its size, and feedback of syndromes into a classical memory, outside the model, can lift this ceiling~\cite{SM}. Without a depolarizing component or with non-Markovian noise~\cite{Sannia2026}, memory can likewise be unbounded~\cite{SM}.
 
